{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff --stat; git diff -- docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc; cat collab/1791210201418-storage-engineer-head-h0-r2.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log collab/1791210371840-storage-engineer-head-h0-r3.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log collab/1791210026362-architect-hydration-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":" docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md | 86 +++++++++++++++++++++++---------\n src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc      | 93 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------\n test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc           | 85 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn   |  2 +-\n 4 files changed, 217 insertions(+), 49 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md\nindex e4688304..6222fffd 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md\n@@ -221,18 +221,33 @@ DHT key (C2).\n   has no trace. The publisher stores no counter.\n - **A reader confirms it.** A loaded index whose greatest `t` is not the\n   trace's sequence refuses the trace, `:yin.head/seq-mismatch`.\n-- **Proven for the REPL path, enforced for every caller.** That two HEAD\n-  writes of one directory never share a greatest `t`, across restart and\n-  `(reset)`, holds for `yin.repl.index` (`index.cljc:189-233, 378-435`)\n-  and H0 pins it. `deposit!` takes the datoms, never a sequence, and\n-  refuses within one process a sequence equal to the last it signed with\n-  another manifest (`:yin.head/seq-collision`) or below it\n-  (`:yin.head/seq-regression`).\n+- **Proven for the REPL path, enforced for every caller.** The HEAD\n+  writes of one directory carry strictly increasing sequences, across\n+  restart and `(reset)`. It holds for `yin.repl.index` because every\n+  publication commits a transaction first (`index.cljc:189-233,\n+  378-435`), and H0 pins it.\n+- `deposit!` takes the datoms, never a sequence, and refuses within one\n+  process a sequence equal to the last it signed with another manifest\n+  (`:yin.head/seq-collision`) or below it (`:yin.head/seq-regression`).\n+- **Hydration is the one HEAD write that commits no transaction, and it\n+  is inside the claim.** It is refused when the directory's HEAD names\n+  another manifest, and it writes nothing when HEAD already names the\n+  same one (`yin/repl/dht.cljc:46-53, 79-82`). So it is only ever a\n+  directory's first HEAD write. Its sequence is the hydrated index's own\n+  greatest `t`, as a restart's recovery has, and the next publication is\n+  one above it.\n+- **The claim is per directory, not per key.** A key that signs from two\n+  directories can sign two manifests at one sequence; readers refuse\n+  them (`:stale`, `:yin.head/equivocation`) and install neither wrongly.\n - **The key kept and the directory lost** restarts the sequence; readers\n   refuse the new traces as stale. The remedy is\n   `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 6.5's, unchanged and still available (5.8):\n   hydrate the directory from the last published manifest before\n-  publishing, or take a new key.\n+  publishing, or take a new key. Only the last published manifest is the\n+  remedy: its trace is the one already signed, a reader judges it\n+  `:duplicate`, and the next round is a candidate. Hydrating an older\n+  manifest, or another publisher's under a key that has published, is the\n+  lost-directory case again.\n \n Rejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter; a `:prev` chain.\n \n@@ -258,7 +273,12 @@ Rejected: the `m` slot (C1); a stored counter; a `:prev` chain.\n   (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5.6). The deposit is a local append.\n - The publisher does not batch; the ring does.\n - At startup a node with a key and a recovered HEAD deposits that HEAD's\n-  trace, so a reader's first contact needs no new round.\n+  trace, so a reader's first contact needs no new round. A completed\n+  hydration counts as that startup: it writes HEAD through the base\n+  store's `:head-fn`, not through the wrapper that calls `announce!`\n+  (`yin/repl/dht.cljc:208`), so the \"deposit after `announce!`\" rule\n+  below does not fire for it. The composition deposits the hydrated\n+  HEAD's trace once, when hydration completes.\n - A node deposits when it has a key, a socket and `:publish?`.\n - **`announce!` and the ledger ring stay as they are, beside the\n   board.** The ledger ring is the node's private work queue of put\n@@ -292,8 +312,8 @@ followed principal, the floor, the installed manifest and the trace:\n \n <table>\n <tr><th>Case</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>\n-<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not a\n-nonnegative integer</td>\n+<tr><td>Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not an\n+integer from 0 to 2^53 - 1</td>\n <td><code>:yin.head/malformed</code></td></tr>\n <tr><td>Principal is not the followed one</td>\n <td><code>:yin.head/wrong-principal</code></td></tr>\n@@ -552,11 +572,12 @@ follows nothing yet.\n \n ClojureDart traps: `heads.edn` is rendered with no whitespace before a\n closing bracket; host branches put `:cljd` first; no protocol method\n-takes `[_ _]`; no test reaches a private var by `#'`; the sequence stays\n-within 2^53 and the vectors include one above 2^32; a manifest address\n-from the wire is compared with `=`; a trace decoded from the channel\n-codec is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes`, asserted on all\n-three hosts.\n+takes `[_ _]`; no test reaches a private var by `#'`; the sequence is at\n+most 2^53 - 1, the greatest integer exact on every host, and a greater\n+one is malformed (5.5), and the vectors include one above 2^32; a\n+manifest address from the wire is compared with `=`; a trace decoded\n+from the channel codec is verified over `dao.jing/canonical-bytes`,\n+asserted on all three hosts.\n \n ## 6. The plain Clojure API\n \n@@ -922,7 +943,9 @@ Node and Dart. H0 and H1 own disjoint source files.\n `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`trace`, `verify`, `seq-of`,\n `judge`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (the head prefix),\n `test/yin/vm/linker/sign_vectors.edn`, new\n-`test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`. Complete when:\n+`test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`, and `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`\n+(the hydration cases; H1 owns that file later and H0 lands first).\n+Complete when:\n - One trace under RFC 8032 TEST 1's seed is in the vectors with its\n   canonical bytes, signed message and signature, byte for byte on all\n   three hosts; one has a sequence above 2^32.\n@@ -933,16 +956,31 @@ Node and Dart. H0 and H1 own disjoint source files.\n - **Sequence zero.** With a `nil` floor a trace of sequence 0 is a\n   candidate. With floor 0: sequence 1 a candidate, sequence 0 with the\n   installed manifest a duplicate, with another `:equivocation`. A\n-  negative or non-integer sequence is `:malformed`. `seq-of` of one\n-  transaction is 0, asserted against `dao.space.transactor`.\n+  negative or non-integer sequence, or one above 2^53 - 1, is\n+  `:malformed`. `seq-of` of one transaction is 0, asserted against\n+  `dao.space.transactor`.\n - `judge` produces every row of 5.5, and its arity admits no source and\n   no candidate.\n+- **Neither `judge` nor `verify` ever throws.** A trace comes off a\n+  channel, so any shape is possible: an envelope or a proof that is a\n+  list of any length, a vector, a number, a string or `nil`, and a\n+  non-map trace, each answer `:yin.head/malformed` (`judge`) and `false`\n+  (`verify`) on every host. The cases are in the tests, odd-length lists\n+  included.\n - `seq-of` over a rehydrated index equals `seq-of` before the restart;\n-  a further round raises it; two HEAD writes of one directory never\n-  share it across rounds, name transactions, `(reset)` and the\n-  unwritten-row recovery of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1. **If a HEAD\n-  move that commits no transaction is found, the slice stops and 5.2 is\n-  reopened.**\n+  a further round raises it; the HEAD writes of one directory carry\n+  strictly increasing sequences across rounds, name transactions,\n+  `(reset)` and the unwritten-row recovery of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md`\n+  5.1.\n+- **Hydration** (5.2), in `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`: into a\n+  directory with no HEAD it writes HEAD once, at the hydrated index's own\n+  sequence, and the reader's next round is one above it; given the\n+  manifest HEAD already names it writes nothing; given another it is\n+  refused and HEAD is unchanged.\n+- **The stop rule.** If any path is found that writes a directory's HEAD\n+  to a manifest whose sequence is not above that directory's previous\n+  HEAD write, the slice stops and 5.2 is reopened. A HEAD write that\n+  commits no transaction but is a directory's first is not that case.\n \n **H1 -- the follower, over any reader.** No transport. Files:\n `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` (`board`, `deposit!`, `follow`,\ndiff --git a/test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc b/test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc\nindex 99c63ddc..a7ac173c 100644\n--- a/test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc\n+++ b/test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc\n@@ -35,6 +35,7 @@\n             [yin.vm.linker :as linker]\n             [yin.vm.linker.closure-test :as ct]\n             [yin.vm.linker.dht :as ld]\n+            [yin.vm.linker.head :as head]\n             [yin.vm.linker.publish :as publish]\n             [yin.vm.linker.publish-test :as publish-test]\n             [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\n@@ -614,6 +615,90 @@\n         (run! cleanup-dir! [pdir rdir])))))\n \n \n+(defn- recorded-dht-store\n+  \"`repl.dht/open` of `spec`, every HEAD write recorded in `heads` after\n+   it succeeds: a round's, through the handle's `:head-fn`, and the\n+   hydration's, through the node's local store (yin.repl.dht/hydrated).\"\n+  [spec heads]\n+  (let [record (fn [head-fn]\n+                 (fn [m] (head-fn m) (swap! heads conj m)))]\n+    (-> (repl.dht/open spec)\n+        (update :head-fn record)\n+        (update-in [:dht :composition :local :head-fn] record))))\n+\n+\n+(deftest hydration-is-a-directory-s-first-head-write-at-its-own-sequence\n+  ;; docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md 5.2: the HEAD writes of one\n+  ;; directory carry strictly increasing sequences; hydration is only ever\n+  ;; the first, installing the index at its own greatest t.\n+  (let [[pdir rdir] [(temp-dir) (temp-dir)]\n+        net (mesh/mesh)\n+        peers {71 (peer-node net 71)}\n+        heads (atom [])\n+        spec (fn [port opts]\n+               (dht-spec rdir (merge {:bind! (mesh-bind net port (atom 0))\n+                                      :peers [{:host \"127.0.0.1\" :port 70}]}\n+                                     opts)))\n+        seq-at (fn [shell m]\n+                 (head/seq-of (space.index/read-datoms (:index-store shell) m)))]\n+    (try\n+      (let [pub (publisher net pdir 70 [71] {:publish? true})\n+            [pub _] (repl/eval-input pub \"(def answer 4242)\")\n+            manifest (get-in pub [:indexer :manifest-address])\n+            published (seq-at pub manifest)\n+            reader (repl/create-state\n+                     {:index-store\n+                      (recorded-dht-store\n+                        (spec 72 {:manifest manifest})\n+                        heads)})\n+            [[pub reader]] (run-until [pub reader] peers 0 20000\n+                                      #(line-with % \"hydrated\"))]\n+        (close! pub)\n+        (testing \"no HEAD: hydration is the one HEAD write, at its own sequence\"\n+          (is (repl.dht/admitting? reader))\n+          (is (= [manifest] @heads))\n+          (is (= published\n+                 (head/seq-of (get-in reader [:index-recovery :datoms])))))\n+        (close! reader)\n+        (testing \"the same manifest: no load, no HEAD write, admitted at once\"\n+          (let [reader (repl/create-state\n+                         {:index-store (recorded-dht-store\n+                                         (spec 73 {:manifest manifest})\n+                                         heads)})]\n+            (is (repl.dht/admitting? reader))\n+            (is (nil? (space.dht/load-status (:dht reader) manifest)))\n+            (is (= [manifest] @heads))\n+            (testing \"one round writes a second HEAD, one sequence above\"\n+              (let [[reader _] (repl/eval-input reader \"(def later 1)\")\n+                    next-manifest (get-in reader\n+                                          [:indexer :manifest-address])\n+                    key (sign/generate)\n+                    p (sign/principal (:public key))]\n+                (is (= [manifest next-manifest] @heads))\n+                (is (= (inc published) (seq-at reader next-manifest)))\n+                (is (= :duplicate\n+                       (head/judge p published manifest\n+                                   (head/trace key manifest published))))\n+                (is (= :candidate\n+                       (head/judge p published manifest\n+                                   (head/trace key next-manifest\n+                                               (inc published)))))))\n+            (close! reader)))\n+        (testing \"a different manifest: refused, HEAD unchanged, lock released\"\n+          (let [held (head-manifest rdir)\n+                other (jing/segment-key \"another index\")\n+                e (refusal-of #(recorded-dht-store\n+                                 (spec 74 {:manifest other})\n+                                 heads))]\n+            (is (some? e))\n+            (is (str/includes? (ex-message e) (str held)))\n+            (is (= held (head-manifest rdir)))\n+            (is (= 2 (count @heads)))\n+            (store/close! (repl.dht/open (spec 75 {}))))))\n+      (finally\n+        (run! cleanup-dir! [pdir rdir])))))\n+\n+\n (deftest a-manifest-no-peer-holds-refuses-the-reader\n   (let [dir (temp-dir)\n         net (mesh/mesh)\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.linker.head\n     2\t  \"The published head trace and its rule (docs/design/\n     3\t   yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md, slice H0): a publisher's index HEAD as a\n     4\t   signed trace, and the pure judgement a reader makes of one.\n     5\t\n     6\t   A trace is exactly the closed shape of 5.2:\n     7\t\n     8\t     {:yin.head/envelope {:yin.head/principal \\\"ed25519:<64 hex>\\\"\n     9\t                          :yin.head/manifest  :segment/...\n    10\t                          :yin.head/seq       n}\n    11\t      :yin.head/proof    {:yin.head/signature \\\"<128 hex>\\\"}}\n    12\t\n    13\t   signed over `yin.head/trace:v1\\\\n` and the envelope's canonical bytes\n    14\t   (`yin.vm.linker.sign/sign-trace`).  The sequence is derived, never\n    15\t   stored: the greatest transaction `t` among the datoms of the index the\n    16\t   manifest names (`seq-of`).\n    17\t\n    18\t   Return shapes this namespace chose where the design leaves them open:\n    19\t   - `judge` answers one keyword, the outcome of the 5.5 table:\n    20\t     `:yin.head/malformed`, `:yin.head/wrong-principal`,\n    21\t     `:yin.head/bad-proof`, `:yin.head/stale`, `:yin.head/equivocation`,\n    22\t     `:duplicate` or `:candidate`.  Its `principal` is the followed\n    23\t     principal id, `\\\"ed25519:\\\"` and 64 lowercase hex; `floor` is a\n    24\t     sequence or nil; `installed` is the installed head's manifest\n    25\t     address, or nil.\n    26\t   - A sequence is a nonnegative integer no greater than 2^53 - 1, the\n    27\t     greatest integer every host holds exactly (5.10: the sequence stays\n    28\t     within 2^53); beyond it a trace is `:yin.head/malformed`.\n    29\t   - `trace` throws `ex-info` when the key or the trace it would build is\n    30\t     not well formed; it never answers a malformed trace.\"\n    31\t  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n    32\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\n    33\t            [dao.space.index :as index]\n    34\t            [yin.vm.linker.sign :as sign]))\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(def ^:private max-seq\n    38\t  \"2^53 - 1: the greatest integer exact on every host.\"\n    39\t  9007199254740991)\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(def ^:private hex-digits \"0123456789abcdef\")\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\t(defn- hex?\n    46\t  [n s]\n    47\t  (and (string? s)\n    48\t       (= n (count s))\n    49\t       (every? #(some? (str/index-of hex-digits (str %))) s)))\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\t(defn- valid-seq?\n    53\t  [n]\n    54\t  (and (integer? n) (<= 0 n max-seq)))\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t(defn- public-of-principal\n    58\t  \"The 64 hex digits of an `ed25519:` principal id, or nil.\"\n    59\t  [principal]\n    60\t  (when (and (string? principal) (str/starts-with? principal \"ed25519:\"))\n    61\t    (let [public (subs principal (count \"ed25519:\"))]\n    62\t      (when (hex? 64 public) public))))\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(defn- well-formed?\n    66\t  \"The closed shape of 5.2, with a sequence that is a nonnegative\n    67\t   integer.\"\n    68\t  [trace]\n    69\t  ;; Each value is checked `map?` with its closed key set before anything\n    70\t  ;; is read from it: a trace comes off a channel and may decode to any\n    71\t  ;; shape.\n    72\t  (try\n    73\t    (boolean\n    74\t      (and (map? trace)\n    75\t           (= #{:yin.head/envelope :yin.head/proof} (set (keys trace)))\n    76\t           (let [envelope (get trace :yin.head/envelope)\n    77\t                 proof (get trace :yin.head/proof)]\n    78\t             (and (map? envelope)\n    79\t                  (= #{:yin.head/principal :yin.head/manifest :yin.head/seq}\n    80\t                     (set (keys envelope)))\n    81\t                  (map? proof)\n    82\t                  (= #{:yin.head/signature} (set (keys proof)))\n    83\t                  (public-of-principal (get envelope :yin.head/principal))\n    84\t                  (jing/segment-address? (get envelope :yin.head/manifest))\n    85\t                  (valid-seq? (get envelope :yin.head/seq))\n    86\t                  (hex? 128 (get proof :yin.head/signature))))))\n    87\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) _ false)))\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(defn trace\n    91\t  \"Build and sign the trace of `manifest` at sequence `n` under `key`,\n    92\t   `{:seed hex :public hex}`.  The principal is the key's own.\"\n    93\t  [key manifest n]\n    94\t  (let [principal (sign/principal (sign/public-of (:seed key)))\n    95\t        envelope {:yin.head/principal principal\n    96\t                  :yin.head/manifest manifest\n    97\t                  :yin.head/seq n}\n    98\t        built {:yin.head/envelope envelope\n    99\t               :yin.head/proof (sign/sign-trace (:seed key) envelope)}]\n   100\t    (when-not (well-formed? built)\n   101\t      (throw (ex-info \"Malformed head trace\"\n   102\t                      {:reason :yin.head/malformed :envelope envelope})))\n   103\t    built))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- proven?\n   107\t  [trace]\n   108\t  (sign/verify-trace (public-of-principal\n   109\t                       (get-in trace [:yin.head/envelope :yin.head/principal]))\n   110\t                     (jing/canonical-bytes (:yin.head/envelope trace))\n   111\t                     (get-in trace [:yin.head/proof :yin.head/signature])))\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(defn verify\n   115\t  \"True when `trace` has the closed shape of 5.2 and its signature is its\n   116\t   principal's head proof over its envelope; false otherwise.  Never\n   117\t   throws.\"\n   118\t  [trace]\n   119\t  (try\n   120\t    (boolean (and (well-formed? trace) (proven? trace)))\n   121\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) _ false)))\n   122\t\n   123\t\n   124\t(defn seq-of\n   125\t  \"The greatest transaction `t` among `datoms`; nil for no datoms.\"\n   126\t  [datoms]\n   127\t  (when (seq datoms)\n   128\t    (reduce max (map index/datom-t datoms))))\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\t(defn judge\n   132\t  \"The rule of 5.5 for one observed trace: a pure function of the\n   133\t   followed `principal`, the `floor` (the installed head's sequence, or\n   134\t   nil), the `installed` manifest and the `trace`.  It takes no source\n   135\t   and no candidate: its answer is the same whoever carried the trace,\n   136\t   in whatever order, and whatever is loading.\"\n   137\t  [principal floor installed trace]\n   138\t  ;; The shape is checked before any field is read: `judge` never throws,\n   139\t  ;; whatever a channel decoded the trace to.\n   140\t  (if-not (well-formed? trace)\n   141\t    :yin.head/malformed\n   142\t    (let [{:yin.head/keys [manifest] n :yin.head/seq}\n   143\t          (:yin.head/envelope trace)]\n   144\t      (cond\n   145\t        (not= principal (:yin.head/principal (:yin.head/envelope trace)))\n   146\t        :yin.head/wrong-principal\n   147\t        (not (verify trace)) :yin.head/bad-proof\n   148\t        (nil? floor) :candidate\n   149\t        (< n floor) :yin.head/stale\n   150\t        (and (= n floor) (= manifest installed)) :duplicate\n   151\t        (= n floor) :yin.head/equivocation\n   152\t        :else :candidate))))\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-05 14:27:26 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 21:27:26 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: c7244636-7620-475e-b23a-a591106058b2\n\nThe hydration test is in `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc` and passes on the JVM. All three cases behaved as the Architect's ruling says.\n\n## What I added (only in that file)\n- **Require:** `[yin.vm.linker.head :as head]` in the ns form.\n- **Helper `recorded-dht-store`:** opens the store with `repl.dht/open` and records each HEAD write after it succeeds. It wraps two places:\n  - the handle's `:head-fn`, which a round's publish uses;\n  - `[:dht :composition :local :head-fn]`, the node's local store, which hydration uses (`yin/repl/dht.cljc:208` calls `(:head-fn (dht/local node))`).\n\n  Wrapping only the handle, as `head_test`'s `recording` does, would miss the hydration write. This helper reaches into the node's internal map (`dao/space/dht.cljc:369-372`).\n- **Test `hydration-is-a-directory-s-first-head-write-at-its-own-sequence`:** a publisher, a mesh peer, and a reader hydrating into an empty directory. No existing test was changed.\n\n## Commands and outcomes\n- `clj -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-test -n yin.vm.linker.head-test -n yin.vm.linker.sign-test`: **54 tests, 529 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.** The new test adds 14 assertions.\n  - The first run had 1 error, in my test setup: `store.cljc:164` refuses a `:manifest` with no peer. I fixed it by giving every reopen the (closed) publisher as a peer.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`: **0 errors, 0 warnings.**\n- No Node or Dart runs and no git commands.\n\n## The three cases\n1. **No HEAD.** As ruled:\n   - After hydration exactly one HEAD write is recorded, the hydrated manifest.\n   - `seq-of` of the reader's `:index-recovery :datoms` equals `seq-of` of the publisher's index.\n   - One later round writes a second HEAD whose sequence is exactly one greater.\n   - A trace of the hydrated manifest at the floor is `:duplicate`, and the next round's trace is `:candidate`.\n\n   The ordering differs from the spec: that round runs in the reader reopened for case 2, not the original session. I did this so case 2 reopens with the manifest HEAD still names. The `heads` record is shared across the reopen.\n2. **Same manifest.** As ruled:\n   - The reader is admitting at once.\n   - `space.dht/load-status` for the manifest is `nil`, so no load started.\n   - No HEAD write is recorded.\n\n   This matches the correction: `open` sets `::hydrating` only when the manifest differs from the recovered one (`yin/repl/dht.cljc:78-82`).\n3. **Different manifest.** As ruled:\n   - `open` throws with a message naming the HEAD the directory holds (`refused-head`, `yin/repl/dht.cljc:46-53, 68-70`).\n   - HEAD on disk is unchanged, and the count of recorded HEAD writes stays 2.\n   - The directory opens again afterwards and is closed, so the lock was released.\n\n## Unresolved\n- Node and Dart are untested for this case; it's written portably and is for your landing lanes.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-05 14:30:18 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 21:30:18 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: c7244636-7620-475e-b23a-a591106058b2\n\nThe `judge` crash is fixed: an odd-length list envelope now answers `:yin.head/malformed`. A new portable regression test shows that no input shape makes `judge` or `verify` throw. The focused JVM tests and kondo are green.\n\n## What changed and why each entry point is now safe\nOnly `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` changed in code.\n\n- **`judge`:** it now runs `(well-formed? trace)` first and answers `:yin.head/malformed` if that fails. It destructures the envelope only after the shape check passes. The old code destructured first; on the JVM, map-destructuring an odd-length seq throws `IllegalArgumentException`. I confirmed that with the old expression on `(list 1 2 3)`. Every case that already worked gives the same answer, since the shape check was already the first branch.\n- **`well-formed?`:** it checks `map?` and the closed key set of the trace before reading the envelope or proof. It then checks `map?` and the key sets of those two before reading any field inside them. A host that decodes a map as something that isn't `map?` gets `false`. The existing `try`/`catch` stays as a backstop.\n- **`verify`:** it reads nested values only after `well-formed?` passes, inside its own `try`.\n- **`trace`:** it only builds maps from its own arguments, then validates. It is meant to throw on bad input, so I left it alone.\n- **`sign.cljc`:** no change was needed.\n  - `verify-trace` and `verify-envelope` do no destructuring, only keyword lookups, all inside their existing `try`/`catch`.\n  - `sign-trace` reads one keyword and, as a signing call, may throw.\n\n## Commands and outcomes\n- `clj -M:test -n yin.vm.linker.head-test -n yin.vm.linker.sign-test`: **13 tests, 220 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.** Last round it was 12 tests and 155 assertions; the new test adds 65.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`: **0 errors, 0 warnings.**\n- No Node or Dart runs and no git commands.\n\n## Regression cases\nNew test `no-shape-makes-judge-or-verify-throw` in `test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`. Each call goes through a helper that turns a throw into a failed assertion, so no exception type is assumed on any host.\n\n- **The envelope**, and separately **the proof**, is each of: an odd-length list, an even-length list, a vector, a number, a string, `nil`, a set, a keyword. That is 16 cases.\n- **The trace itself** is `nil`, a number, a vector, a list or a string. That is 5 cases.\n- For each of the 21 cases:\n  - `judge` answers `:yin.head/malformed` with the floor and installed manifest both `nil`, and again with floor 0 and the installed manifest set.\n  - `verify` answers `false`.\n- **Kept explicitly:** the case that threw before the fix, an odd-length list envelope.\n- **Control:** a good trace still judges `:duplicate`.\n\n## Unresolved\n- **Bad caller arguments can still throw.** `judge` checks only the trace. A non-number `floor` passed by the caller would still throw at `<`. That argument is the caller's own state, not channel input, so I left it unchecked; say if you want it guarded too.\n- **Node and Dart are untested** for the new cases; they're for your landing lanes.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-05 14:22:29 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-05 21:22:29 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 06a2b171-f2e5-47cd-8b2a-49779ebcbeb5\n\n**Ruling: outside the shared claim. 5.2 is not reopened, the derived sequence stands, and H0 resumes.** Hydration is a HEAD write that commits no transaction, so the engineer was right to stop on the literal wording. But the stop rule guards one property: the HEAD writes of one directory carry strictly increasing sequences. Hydration cannot break it, because it is only ever the first HEAD write of a directory, and it installs an index at that index's own greatest `t`, exactly as restart recovery does. The design's wording is what is defective. No file was edited and nothing was run; this is from reading the design and the code.\n\n## Findings\n\nSeverity | file:line | invariant / evidence | recommended correction\n\n1. **Medium (design wording)** | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:943-945` | The stop rule says \"a HEAD move that commits no transaction\", but the property it protects is strict increase per directory (5.2, lines 224-227). The design already knew of this HEAD write: D1 cites `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:202-212` by line and says \"Hydration itself is left alone\" (lines 91-97), and 5.8 keeps it deliberately (lines 512-518). | Replace the stop rule with the text in \"H0 text\" below.\n\n2. **None (code is sound)** | `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:46-53, 68-70, 79-82, 202-212` | Three cases, all safe within one directory:\n   - **Different manifest:** `open` throws before the node is joined, so HEAD is not written.\n   - **Same manifest:** the `cond->` at lines 79-82 never sets `::hydrating`, so `hydrated` never runs and HEAD is not written. The engineer's analysis says hydration \"runs\" here; it does not.\n   - **No HEAD:** hydration is the directory's first HEAD write. `index/rehydrate` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:189-219`) seeds the log with every datom at its original `t`, and `derive-next-t` (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:117-131`) gives the next transaction greatest `t` plus 1. Evaluation is not admitted until it completes (`dht.cljc:283-289`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc:827`).\n   \n   | No code change.\n\n3. **Low (design wording)** | `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.head.md:231-235` | The engineer's cross-directory analysis is correct. The claim is per directory, and a key used over two directories is the case 5.2 already names. Readers stay safe: they answer `:stale` or `:yin.head/equivocation` and never install a wrong head. Hydrating from the last published manifest re-signs the same envelope at the same sequence, which a reader judges `:duplicate`, and the next round is a candidate. That is why it is the remedy. Hydrating from an older manifest, or a foreign one under a key that has published elsewhere, is not the remedy. | Add the 5.2 text below.\n\n4. **Low (note for the slice that composes `deposit!`, not H0)** | `src/cljc/yin/repl/dht.cljc:208` against `:84-86` | Hydration writes HEAD through the base store's `:head-fn`, not the wrapper that calls `announce!`. A deposit composed \"after `announce!`\" (5.4, lines 263-266) will therefore not fire for hydration. | Treat a completed hydration as 5.4's startup deposit (line 260): deposit the hydrated HEAD's trace once, when hydration completes. One sentence in 5.4, in the slice that composes the publisher.\n\n5. **Confirmed, with a table row** | `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc:37-54`; design lines 295-297, 555-556, 936 | The bound of 2^53 - 1 inclusive is correct, and it is tighter than 5.10's \"within 2^53\" for a reason. 2^53 itself is exact on JS, but 2^53 + 1 decodes to 2^53 there, so a JS reader could not tell the two signed sequences apart. Only values up to 2^53 - 1 are unambiguous on every host. `:malformed` is the right outcome, since it is a shape check made before the principal and the proof. | Keep the code. Amend the 5.5 row, 5.10 and the H0 criterion as below.\n\n## Exact text\n\n**5.2**, replacing the bullet at lines 224-230 up to \"H0 pins it.\":\n\n> - **Proven for the REPL path, enforced for every caller.** The HEAD writes of one directory carry strictly increasing sequences, across restart and `(reset)`. It holds for `yin.repl.index` because every publication commits a transaction first (`index.cljc:189-233, 378-435`), and H0 pins it.\n> - **Hydration is the one HEAD write that commits no transaction, and it is inside the claim.** It is refused when the directory's HEAD names another manifest, and it writes nothing when HEAD already names the same one (`yin/repl/dht.cljc:46-53, 79-82`). So it is only ever a directory's first HEAD write. Its sequence is the hydrated index's own greatest `t`, as a restart's recovery has, and the next publication is one above it.\n> - **The claim is per directory, not per key.** A key that signs from two directories can sign two manifests at one sequence; readers refuse them (`:stale`, `:yin.head/equivocation`) and install neither wrongly.\n\nAppend to the \"key kept and the directory lost\" bullet (line 235):\n\n> Only the last published manifest is the remedy: its trace is the one already signed, a reader judges it `:duplicate`, and the next round is a candidate. Hydrating an older manifest, or another publisher's under a key that has published, is the lost-directory case again.\n\n**H0 text**, replacing the last criterion (lines 940-945):\n\n> - `seq-of` over a rehydrated index equals `seq-of` before the restart; a further round raises it; the HEAD writes of one directory carry strictly increasing sequences across rounds, name transactions, `(reset)` and the unwritten-row recovery of `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.1.\n> - **Hydration** (5.2), in `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`: into a directory with no HEAD it writes HEAD once, at the hydrated index's own sequence, and the reader's next round is one above it; given the manifest HEAD already names it writes nothing; given another it is refused and HEAD is unchanged.\n> - **The stop rule.** If any path is found that writes a directory's HEAD to a manifest whose sequence is not above that directory's previous HEAD write, the slice stops and 5.2 is reopened. A HEAD write that commits no transaction but is a directory's first is not that case.\n\nAlso add `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc` to H0's file list. H1 owns that file later, and H0 lands first, so there is no conflict.\n\n**5.5 table, first row** (lines 295-297):\n\n> Not the closed shape of 5.2, or a sequence that is not an integer from 0 to 2^53 - 1\n\n**5.10** (lines 555-556): change \"the sequence stays within 2^53\" to \"the sequence is at most 2^53 - 1, the greatest integer exact on every host, and a greater one is malformed (5.5)\".\n\n**H0 sequence-zero criterion** (line 936): change \"A negative or non-integer sequence\" to \"A negative or non-integer sequence, or one above 2^53 - 1,\".\n\n## The hydration test\n\nYes, H0 should add it. It needs the mesh harness, so it goes in `test/yin/repl/dht_test.cljc`, extending the setup of `a-reader-given-a-manifest-hydrates-then-queries-the-remote-index` (lines 584-614). Record HEAD writes as `head_test.cljc`'s `recording` does (lines 293-298), and assert:\n\n1. **No HEAD:**\n   - Exactly one HEAD write, the manifest.\n   - `seq-of` of the reader's recovered datoms equals `seq-of` of the publisher's index.\n   - One reader round then writes a second HEAD whose sequence is exactly one greater.\n   - A trace of the hydrated manifest at that sequence is judged `:duplicate` against floor = that sequence, and the next round's trace `:candidate`.\n2. **Same manifest:** reopening the hydrated directory with the same `:manifest` admits evaluation at once, starts no load and records zero HEAD writes.\n3. **Different manifest:** `open` throws naming the HEAD it holds, HEAD on disk is unchanged, zero HEAD writes are recorded, and the directory opens again afterwards (the lock was released).\n\nThe existing `seq-of-across-head-moves` and `seq-of-across-the-unwritten-row-recovery` already cover the rest of the criterion and need no change.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
